-*🇬🇧* To address these issues, we propose a multi-task framework for blind $T_{60}$ estimation that pairs $T_{60}$ regression with a denoising reconstruction auxiliary task over a shared time--frequency Conformer feature extractor. The denoising task provides dense per-time--frequency-bin supervision, while gradient-probe and representation analyses examine how this supervision affects the shared features and the interaction between the two tasks. We further use a Fourier-KAN regression head to provide a nonlinear readout from the shared features to $T_{60}$, and compare it with an MLP head under the same multi-task setting.
